# Deliverable Sweep + Ledger [SPEC:CXC-DS]

> The deliverable-focused sibling of `python/composeio/session_sweep/` (undone WORK) and
> `python/composeio/compact_summary.py` (deterministic mechanical extraction). Where session_sweep asks "what
> got left on the floor this session," deliverable_sweep asks "what non-code artifact appeared, changed, or
> vanished since the last sweep" — complementary loose-ends-vs-artifacts detectors, same deterministic-no-LLM
> idiom, same `--selftest` discipline. The perceive/act split (Problem 1) is the spine: this app owns every
> field a script can COMPUTE from the filesystem; it stamps `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` on the seven fields it cannot
> and never guesses them.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-DS-INTENT] The Cheapest Node Fully Owns the Mechanical Check

- definition: a deterministic python app sweeps one or more project roots for non-code artifacts, maintains a
  single ledger `.lts`, and reports the delta (NEW / MODIFIED / REMOVED) since the last run. It never invokes
  a reasoning model; every field it writes is reproducible from the filesystem + the prior ledger state.
- rationale: matches the compact_summary precedent — "extracting it is a grep, not a reasoning task; don't
  spend an Opus turn transcribing what a 30-line script copies exactly." Here the mechanical residue is larger
  (path/format/hash/diff/history) but the principle is identical: the reasoning layer's job shrinks to exactly
  the fields it uniquely can supply.
- status: planned
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-SIBLING-DISTINCTION] session_sweep = undone WORK; deliverable_sweep = non-code ARTIFACTS

- definition: `session_sweep` parses a session `.jsonl` and detectors surface things a party STARTED and
  DROPPED (uncommitted authored files, unanswered questions, unhandled errors) — a *process* signal, scoped to
  one session. `deliverable_sweep` walks the FILESYSTEM (not a transcript) and detects artifacts that EXIST —
  a *state* signal, scoped to however many sessions since the last sweep. Complementary, not overlapping: a
  session can end with zero loose ends (session_sweep silent) yet still have produced three new deliverables
  (deliverable_sweep loud), or vice versa.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

---

## 2. The Perceive/Act Split (load-bearing — restated precisely for this build)

### [CXC-DS-PORTABILITY] Extraction-Clean: a Generic FactLattice Tool, Not Composeio-Specific

- definition: David's intent is for this tool to PUBLISH to the FactLattice repo (740Dave/factlattice) once
  proven here — any domain keeping a deliverable-ledger uses it, not just ComposeIO. So the implementation
  package (`python/composeio/deliverable_sweep/`) imports NOTHING from composeio internals — stdlib plus
  PyYAML only — and invents no composeio-specific default: `project-roots`, every filter list, the ledger
  path, and even the frontmatter's `domain`/`confidentiality`/`artifact-readiness` are 100% config/CLI-driven
  (`--root`, `--ledger`, `--domain`, ...), never hardcoded. It develops and is proven HERE, against this
  repo's real artifacts (the dry-run in the build summary), but nothing in the code assumes it's running in
  ComposeIO specifically. session_sweep/compact_summary are studied for their IDIOMS (the walk/CLI/selftest
  shape), never imported.
- test: `grep -rn "^import composeio\|^from composeio" python/composeio/deliverable_sweep/` returns nothing;
  `DEFAULT_FRONTMATTER` carries no domain-specific value, only the universal `machine-maintained: true`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-FIELD-OWNERSHIP] Mechanical vs. Semantic Field Sets

- mechanical (app-owned, always written/updated):
  `path`, `project-root`, `format`, `created` (first-seen; set ONCE, never touched again), `modified` (bumped
  only when content-hash changes), `hash` (`sha256:<hex>` of file bytes), `missing` (bool — see §4), plus the
  node id and the run-history/diff bookkeeping.
- semantic (Intelligence-owned, NEVER computed by the app): exactly the seven fields named in
  `docs/deliverable-sweep/GUIDELINES.md` — `kind`, `topics`, `summary`, `target-audience`, `confidentiality`,
  `review-outcome`, `lifecycle-position`. On a NEW node the app stamps all seven to the literal string
  `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT`. On MODIFIED the app leaves them exactly as they were (a content change doesn't
  invalidate a prior human verdict automatically — that's also an Intelligence call, made visible via the
  MODIFIED report entry, not auto-reset by the app).
- conditionally-mechanical: `generated-in-session` — the proposal schema marks it required, but the sweep has
  no transcript access (unlike session_sweep) and cannot know which session authored a pre-existing file. The
  app writes it ONLY if the caller passes `--session <key>` (the bootstrap/session-end harness DOES know the
  current session key and can supply it for artifacts it itself just caused to appear); absent that flag the
  app stamps `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` like the other semantic fields, rather than guessing.
- `pending-enrichment` — an additional MECHANICAL bookkeeping property, not itself one of the seven semantic
  fields: a flow-list of exactly which enrichable field names (the seven semantic fields +
  `generated-in-session`) currently carry the literal `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` sentinel, e.g.
  `pending-enrichment: [topics, summary, lifecycle-position]`. Computing it is a pure string-equality scan —
  no interpretation, so writing it never crosses the perceive/act line. The app (re)stamps it whenever it
  already touches a node (on NEW creation, and again on every MODIFIED touch, recomputed from the node's
  CURRENT field values) — so a node an Intelligence only PARTIALLY hand-enriched self-heals to an accurate
  worklist the next time a real content change brings the sweep back to it. This turns "which nodes still
  await enrichment, and on which exact fields" into one query over `## Deliverables`, per the coordinator's
  base-spec-alignment ask — the ledger-level `needs-enrichment` id list in the `--json` report (§8) is the
  same computation at the whole-run granularity.
- test: a synthetic NEW file's `pending-enrichment` lists all eight enrichable field names; hand-setting `kind`
  and `summary` to real values, then triggering a real MODIFIED (a content change), recomputes
  `pending-enrichment` to the remaining six — `kind`/`summary` absent, everything still-`NEEDS-ENRICHMENT`
  present.
- test: a synthetic NEW file, swept with no `--session` flag, has all eight of
  {kind, topics, summary, target-audience, confidentiality, review-outcome, lifecycle-position,
  generated-in-session} == `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT`, and all of {path, project-root, format, created, hash} populated
  and correct.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-REMOVED-NOT-DELETED] A REMOVED Artifact Is Flagged, Never Deleted

- definition: when a ledger node's `path` no longer resolves on disk, the app does NOT delete the node and
  does NOT touch `lifecycle-position` (that field is semantic — off-limits even here). It sets the purely
  computable fact `missing: true` and `missing-since: <date>` (mtime-free; date the sweep first observed the
  absence — an injected "now", see [CXC-DS-DETERMINISM]). The REMOVED finding is reported every run until an
  Intelligence resolves it (typically by setting `lifecycle-position: superseded` or `withdrawn` by hand, at
  which point it's no longer a live concern — though the app keeps reporting `missing: true` regardless, since
  that's still a true mechanical fact; only the *severity* of caring about it is an Intelligence/human call).
  If a path with an existing `missing: true` node reappears, the app clears `missing` to `false`, updates
  `hash`/`modified`, and reports it as MODIFIED (a restoration), not NEW — the node id (stable, path-derived)
  never changes.
- change: "Resolved the guidelines' apparent tension: GUIDELINES.md §2 says a removed artifact may be marked
  `lifecycle-position: superseded` OR a `missing: true` flag; but the load-bearing principle says the app NEVER
  writes `lifecycle-position` under any circumstance. This spec picks `missing: true` exclusively as the
  app-owned removal marker and leaves `lifecycle-position` strictly to the Intelligence, with no exception for
  removal."
  context_for_change:
    prior_state: "GUIDELINES.md offered both markers as options without resolving the ownership conflict."
    reasoning: "The perceive/act split is explicitly called load-bearing and 'do not blur it' — carving out a
      removal-only exception for lifecycle-position would blur exactly the line the brief insists on holding.
      missing:true is 100% computable (a stat() that fails); lifecycle-position:superseded is a judgment
      (superseded BY what? is it actually gone, or just relocated? does David still want it?) — that judgment
      belongs to the Intelligence."
    rejected_alternative: "Let the app write lifecycle-position:superseded directly on removal — rejected
      because it's indistinguishable from the app GUESSING a semantic verdict, the exact anti-pattern the
      brief warns against."
    invalidating_assumption: "If a future revision of the base deliverable-node schema demotes
      lifecycle-position to a computable/enumerable state machine (e.g. purely derived from missing + a small
      number of mechanical rules), this decision should be reopened — today it's an open judgment call."
    implication: "A REMOVED artifact stays visibly NEEDS-ENRICHMENT-adjacent (missing:true, lifecycle-position
      whatever it last was) until an Intelligence actively resolves it; the sweep's job is only to keep
      surfacing it, per David's explicit ask that removals be surfaced, not silently resolved."
- test: delete a previously-swept file, re-run `--write`; the node persists in the ledger with `missing: true`,
  `missing-since` set, `lifecycle-position` UNCHANGED from its pre-removal value, and it appears in the
  REMOVED list of the report. Restore the file (same bytes or different) and re-run; `missing` clears to
  `false`, the node appears in MODIFIED (not NEW), and the node id is unchanged.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

---

## 3. The Ledger `.lts` Format ("one-stop-shopping")

### [CXC-DS-LEDGER-LOCATION] `corpora/deliverables/deliverable-ledger.lts`

- definition: a single file — its own queryable corpus directory under `corpora/` — holding, in one
  document, the config block, every `[DELIV-*]` node (live and `missing`), the run-history event stream, and
  a latest-run findings summary. One file to read for "what does the project have, and what changed." (It
  formerly lived at the top-level `deliverables/deliverable-ledger.lts`; the corpus consolidation moved it
  under `corpora/` so the lts-engine loads it whole with no `only_file` workaround.)
- machine-owned: like the decision-graph, this file is written by an Intelligence composing prose for the
  human-authored sections (none here) and by THIS APP for every structural section — but unlike a decision
  graph, 100% of this file's structure is machine-generated/machine-maintained; there is no hand-authored prose
  layer. `--write` performs a full read-modify-write: parse existing ledger -> compute delta -> merge -> render
  -> save. Re-running `--write` with no filesystem change re-renders byte-identical output (determinism, §7).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-LEDGER-LOCATION-SINGLE-SOURCE] The Ledger Location Is Resolved Through One Constant, Never a Literal

- definition: EVERY consumer that opens, writes, watches, or projects the ledger — the batch `deliverable_sweep`,
  the transcript `deliverable_monitor`, the filesystem `deliverable_watcher`, the `deliverable_publisher`
  projection, and the in-process console data layer — resolves the ledger path from the ONE source of truth,
  `composeio.bootstrap_paths.DELIVERABLE_LEDGER` (absolute) / `DELIVERABLE_LEDGER_REL` (repo-relative). No
  consumer may carry a hard-coded ledger path literal.
- invariant: a `--write` (batch or realtime) MUST NOT create or update a ledger at any path other than the one
  the single source resolves to. A path literal that survives a relocation is a DEFECT of the exact class the
  bootstrap-paths module exists to eliminate ([SPEC:CXC-IC-BOOTSTRAP-PATHS]).
- rationale: `Ledger.load` of a NON-EXISTENT path returns an EMPTY ledger — by design, so a first run bootstraps
  cleanly. The hazard: a consumer pointed at a STALE location therefore loads nothing, classes every tracked
  artifact as NEW, and writes a fresh all-`NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` ledger THERE. That does two kinds of damage —
  (1) a placeholder "ghost" ledger materializes at the retired path (freshly created, mode 0600 from the atomic
  temp-file write), and (2) if that placeholder output is ever copied/committed onto the real corpus path, it
  clobbers curated enrichment. The preservation logic in [CXC-DS-FIELD-OWNERSHIP] is NOT the failure here — it
  works whenever the CORRECT ledger is loaded; the failure is loading the WRONG (empty) file, so there was never
  any enrichment to preserve. Single-source resolution is the guard that makes both impossible.
- test: no runtime `.py` under `python/composeio` or `intelligence` carries the retired
  `deliverables/deliverable-ledger.lts` literal (the `corpora/`-rooted canonical form is the only permitted
  spelling) — enforced red in `python/tests/render/test_bootstrap_paths_single_source.py`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-LEDGER-SECTIONS] The Four Sections, In Order

1. **Frontmatter** — standard FactLattice document classification (`domain`, `confidentiality`,
   `artifact-readiness`, `created` — all OPTIONAL, seeded only via `--domain`/`--confidentiality`/
   `--artifact-readiness` on first creation, per [CXC-DS-PORTABILITY]; this tool invents no domain-specific
   default) PLUS one universal marker every ledger carries regardless of domain: **`machine-maintained:
   true`** — the base-spec convention (coordinator alignment, 2026-07-20) signaling a TOOL owns this file:
   don't hand-edit its structure, don't lint it as an authored spec. Frontmatter is NOT the sweep config
   (that's its own section, since it's operational data — roots/globs/lists — not document classification
   metadata).
2. **`## Config`** — a single fenced `yaml` block: `project-roots` (list), `include-globs` (list, POSIX-style,
   relative to each root), `exclude-globs` (list, override include), `do-not-track-dirs` (list of directory
   names, matched anywhere in the path), `do-not-track-suffixes` (list of filename suffixes). This is the ONLY
   section a human/architect hand-tunes (edit the yaml block directly, or re-dispatch the app with different
   `--root`/`--ledger` flags which seed a fresh block on first `--write`).
3. **`## Deliverables`** — one `### [DELIV-<slug>-<hash8>] <path>` node per artifact ever seen (live or
   `missing`), each followed by a flat `- key: value` property list. Property order is FIXED (mechanical first,
   then semantic, alphabetical within each group) so unrelated re-renders diff cleanly.
4. **`## Run History`** — a TEMPORAL EVENT-STREAM (the base-spec term of art, SPEC.md §11's JSONL event-stream
   layer, not a bespoke log format): a fenced `jsonl` block, one `core.entry`-shaped line per sweep run
   (append-only — the app appends a line on every `--write`, never rewrites prior lines), each carrying a
   timestamp plus the `{new, modified, removed}` counts plus the affected node ids: `{"type":
   "deliverable_sweep.run", "id": "RUN-<iso>", "date": iso, "new": N, "modified": N, "removed": N, "new_ids":
   [...], "modified_ids": [...], "removed_ids": [...]}`. Matches SPEC.md §11.1's `core.entry` base class,
   domain-extended as `deliverable_sweep.run`.
5. **`## Findings — Latest Run`** — human-readable rollup, WHOLLY REGENERATED on every `--write` (it is a
   projection of the last run-history entry + current node states, not itself source of truth — the app never
   parses this section back in).
- test: round-trip — load a hand-seeded ledger, run `--write` with zero filesystem changes, diff the file
  before/after: byte-identical except (a) a new Run History line with all-zero counts and (b) the Findings
  section timestamp. Property order in every `### [DELIV-*]` block is stable across repeated writes.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-BASE-SPEC-ALIGNMENT] Naming Chosen to Match the Upstream Base-Spec Proposal

- change: "Adopted `machine-maintained: true`, `pending-enrichment: [field, ...]`, and the explicit
  'temporal event-stream' framing for Run History, as the exact vocabulary — not synonyms — this spec + its
  implementation use."
  context_for_change:
    prior_state: "The first draft of this spec already had the equivalent BEHAVIOR (a machine-owned-file
      convention in the frontmatter intro prose, a NEEDS-ENRICHMENT sentinel, a JSONL run-history) but under
      ad hoc names, not names chosen to match anything upstream."
    reasoning: "The coordinator is authoring the companion base-spec proposal for FactLattice itself
      (740Dave/factlattice) and needs this build's concrete vocabulary to match it exactly, so the base spec
      and this concrete ledger agree on wire format without a translation layer. Since this tool is also
      slated to PUBLISH into that same repo (a generic, domain-agnostic FactLattice-ecosystem tool — see
      [CXC-DS-PORTABILITY]), matching names now avoids a rename-everywhere later."
    rejected_alternative: "Keep the original prose-only 'don't hand-edit this' marker instead of a formal
      frontmatter key — rejected because a prose convention isn't machine-queryable; a linter or another tool
      can't cheaply detect 'is this file machine-maintained' from prose the way it can from a boolean key."
    invalidating_assumption: "If the upstream base-spec proposal lands with DIFFERENT key names than what's
      used here, this is a one-line rename in ledger.py's FIELD_ORDER/DEFAULT_FRONTMATTER — not a redesign,
      per the schema-tolerant discipline already load-bearing throughout this spec."
    implication: "This spec and 740Dave/factlattice's eventual base-spec text should be read together; this
      document is the reference implementation of the convention, not just a consumer of it."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-NODE-ID] Deterministic Node Identity

- definition: `id = "DELIV-" + slug(basename_without_ext) + "-" + sha256(project-root + "::" + relpath)[:8]`.
  The slug makes the id human-scannable; the hash8 suffix guarantees no two distinct paths collide and makes
  the id STABLE across runs for the same path (content changes do NOT change the id — only path identity does)
  so MODIFIED is "same id, new hash," not a delete+recreate.
- test: sweeping the same file twice (unchanged) yields the identical id both times; two different files with
  the same basename in different directories get different ids; renaming a file is NOT detected as
  modified-in-place (a rename is a REMOVED old-path + NEW new-path — the sweep has no rename-detection heuristic
  by design, since "is this a rename or an unrelated new file" is exactly the kind of guess the perceive/act
  split forbids the app from making; an Intelligence can later link them via a `supersedes` edge).
- canonical-form: the emitted id is UPPERCASE (`(...).upper()`), because the FactLattice node-id grammar is
  `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_\-]*$` (`factlattice/parser.py::_UPPER_ID_RE`): a lowercased slug is SILENTLY skipped by the
  parser, making the whole ledger invisible to lts-engine. The human-readable path is the node TITLE, so nothing
  legible is lost. This is the ONE canonical spelling of a DELIV-id; a legacy lowercase-slug id is a case-VARIANT
  of it, never a distinct node.
- identity-reconcile — DEDUP ON IDENTITY, NOT THE ID STRING: the artifact's identity is (project-root, path),
  NOT the derived id text. Before diffing, the sweep RE-KEYS every stored node to the id its own
  (project-root, path) derives under the CURRENT derivation, and MERGES (enrichment-preserving) any two nodes
  that collapse to one identity. So a change in the derivation's case/format — the historical lowercase->uppercase
  migration, or any future slug/hash8 change — UPDATES the existing node in place and NEVER mints a case-variant
  twin. This closes the recurrence where keying the diff on the id STRING alone appended ~66 bare-`NEEDS-
  ENRICHMENT` duplicates beside their already-enriched originals and tombstoned the originals as `missing`
  (~doubling a real ledger). Merge precedence: a LIVE node beats a `missing` one; else the NEWER `modified` wins
  (a hash-drift pair keeps the CURRENT-hash version, drops the stale one); else the MORE-ENRICHED node wins (a
  real semantic value is never dropped for a bare placeholder twin).
- test (identity-reconcile): a ledger holding a node under an OLD-format id for a live path, re-swept, yields
  exactly ONE node for that path — re-keyed to the canonical id, all semantic fields intact, reported MODIFIED/
  unchanged, never NEW; and no two nodes in a saved ledger are ever case-variants of one another.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

