## 4. Filters — the only thing between a useful ledger and noise

### [CXC-DS-FILTER-DEFAULTS] Tuned Defaults for THIS Repo (portable pattern, not hardcoded paths)

- include-globs (POSIX fnmatch, relative to project-root):
  `docs/**/*.md`, `docs/**/*.html`, `docs/**/*.svg`, `docs/**/*.pdf`, `decisions/**/*.lts`.
- exclude-globs (checked AFTER include, so they win even inside an included directory):
  `**/*.py`, `**/*.js`, `**/*.ts`, `**/*.tsx`, `**/*.jsx`, `**/*.mjs`, `**/*.cjs`, `**/*.cpp`, `**/*.c`,
  `**/*.h`, `**/*.hpp`, `**/*.go`, `**/*.rs`, `**/*.java`, `**/*.sh`, `**/*.ino`, `**/*.yaml`, `**/*.yml`,
  `**/*.json`.
- do-not-track-dirs (matched as a path component anywhere under a project root — hard stop, never walked
  into): `.git`, `node_modules`, `__pycache__`, `.pio`, `.pytest_cache`, `.mypy_cache`, `.venv`, `venv`, `env`,
  `build`, `dist`, `.eggs`, `site`, `.ray`, `_generated`, `.claude`.
- do-not-track-suffixes: `.pyc`, `.pyo`, `.egg-info`.
- secrets (belt-and-suspenders — these overlap with a repo's own `.gitignore` secrets block by design, e.g.
  this repo's `**/wifi_config.h`, `**/secrets.yaml`, `**/*.creds`, `**/*.key`, `**/*.pem`, `**/.env`): treated
  as additional do-not-track-suffixes/names, never as include-globs regardless of extension (a `secrets.yaml`
  would otherwise never match `*.yaml` anyway since that's excluded, but the explicit list guards a future
  include-glob widening from ever re-admitting them).
- change: "Scoped the `.lts`/`.smd` positive filter to `decisions/**/*.lts` specifically, NOT every `.lts`/
  `.smd` file in the repo (which would also sweep in `ux_composer/specs/*.lts` and `spec/*.smd`)."
  context_for_change:
    prior_state: "GUIDELINES.md's acceptance bar names decisions/*.lts as a required find but is silent on
      the ux_composer/specs and spec/ corpora."
    reasoning: "Those corpora are the living, actively-iterated SYSTEM SPECIFICATION — versioned and edited
      much like source code (by an Intelligence composing FactLattice, but iteratively, not as one-shot review
      artifacts). The node-kind-proposal's own framing is 'an artifact GENERATED FOR REVIEW... distinct from
      the decisions they serve and from source code' — a spec corpus reads as source-of-truth architecture,
      not a one-off deliverable. decisions/*.lts is different in kind: it's a ledger/log itself, the same
      shape as this ledger, explicitly named in the brief."
    rejected_alternative: "Include `**/*.lts` and `**/*.smd` repo-wide — rejected: this repo alone has ~43
      such files today (23 under spec/, 20 under ux_composer/specs/) that would dominate the ledger with
      noise the brief never asked to see, undermining the 'the only thing between a useful ledger and noise'
      framing."
    invalidating_assumption: "If David starts treating individual ux_composer/specs entries as one-shot review
      deliverables (rather than a living corpus) — e.g. wants a history of 'spec 37 as submitted for review' —
      this scoping should be revisited; a project-root-level include-glob is a one-line config edit either
      way, not a code change (schema-tolerant filters, not hardcoded rejection)."
    implication: "Root-level project docs (BACKLOG.md, BOOTSTRAP.md, etc.), reviews/, competitive_analysis/,
      and spec-impl-gap-reports/ are also excluded by the same 'docs/** + decisions/**' scoping — not because
      they're uninteresting, but because the default is deliberately narrow and precise against the acceptance
      bar; widening is a Config-block edit, never a rewrite."
- test: given this repo, the tuned defaults hit every item in GUIDELINES.md's acceptance list (dusk-gift
  primers, console previs `*.html`, docs/ reports, decisions/*.lts) and exclude every `.py` file including
  `docs/intelligence-console/session_sensor.py` (a real confound: a code file living INSIDE an included
  directory — proves exclude-globs win over include-globs, not just directory-level filtering).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-FILTER-PRECEDENCE] Evaluation Order

- definition: for each file under a project root: (1) if any path component matches a do-not-track-dir, or the
  filename matches a do-not-track-suffix, SKIP (do not even stat/hash it — cheapest check first); (2) if the
  relative path matches any exclude-glob, SKIP; (3) if the relative path matches any include-glob, KEEP;
  (4) else SKIP (default-exclude — the whitelist is authoritative, not "everything not explicitly banned").
- test: a file matching BOTH an include-glob and an exclude-glob is excluded (exclude wins); a file under a
  do-not-track dir that would otherwise match an include-glob is skipped before the glob check ever runs.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

