## 5. Sweep Semantics

### [CXC-DS-DIFF] New / Modified / Removed, Precisely

- NEW: a file matches the filters, resolves to a node id not present in the ledger (or present with
  `missing: true` and now reappeared under a DIFFERENT id — impossible by construction, ids are path-derived,
  so a reappearance under the SAME path is always the "restoration" MODIFIED case, never NEW).
- MODIFIED: a file's node id exists in the ledger, the file exists on disk, and its content hash differs from
  the stored `hash` (OR the node was `missing: true` and the path now resolves again — a restoration, also
  reported as MODIFIED). `modified` is set to the file's current mtime (ISO date). Semantic fields untouched.
- REMOVED: a node id exists in the ledger with `missing` unset/false, and its `path` no longer resolves under
  any configured project-root. See [CXC-DS-REMOVED-NOT-DELETED].
- UNCHANGED: everything else — not reported, not touched (byte-identical node in the rewritten ledger).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-DETERMINISM] No Scattered Wall-Clock

- definition: `created` and `modified` are ALWAYS derived from the swept file's own mtime (`os.stat().st_mtime`
  -> ISO date), never from `time.time()` at sweep-time — two sweeps of an unchanged tree at different real
  wall-clock moments produce byte-identical `created`/`modified` values. The ONE wall-clock read per invocation
  is the run-history entry's `date` (and `missing-since` for a newly-detected removal) — both sourced from a
  single injected `now` value (an ISO-8601 string), defaulting to real UTC now but overridable
  (`Sweep(..., now="2026-07-20T00:00:00Z")`) so `--selftest` and any other caller gets fully reproducible
  output without monkeypatching `time`.
- test: two sweeps of an identical synthetic tree with the same injected `now` and the same starting ledger
  produce byte-identical rendered ledger text (see [CXC-DS-LEDGER-SECTIONS] round-trip test) and byte-identical
  `--json` report output.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-DRY-RUN] `--write` Is Opt-In; Default Is a Safe Read-Only Report

- definition: without `--write`, the app loads the ledger (or treats it as empty if absent), computes the
  delta against the CURRENT filesystem, and prints the report — it writes NOTHING, not even a run-history
  line. This is the safe mode for bootstrap display (the harness runs the sweep, hands the Intelligence the
  delta, and only a LATER explicit `--write` — or the session-end integration — persists it). `--write`
  performs the diff AND persists: new nodes (mechanical + NEEDS-ENRICHMENT stamps), modified nodes' `hash`/
  `modified`, removed nodes' `missing`/`missing-since`, plus one new run-history line and a regenerated
  Findings section.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

---

## 5a. Forward Compat: a Future Live Monitor Shares This Ledger

### [CXC-DS-MULTI-WRITER-SAFE] Mergeable at Node Granularity, Not Built Here

- definition: David is planning a companion LIVE monitor (tails the session transcript `.jsonl` for doc-writes,
  appends to the ledger in real time, emits a `deliverable/new` bus alert) that will write the SAME
  `deliverable-ledger.lts` this batch sweep writes. This spec does not build that monitor, but the ledger
  format and this app's write path are designed so a future single-node live append and this app's full-tree
  reconcile can coexist without a rewrite:
  1. **Path-keyed identity already holds.** [CXC-DS-NODE-ID]'s id is a pure function of `project-root` +
     relative `path` — never a random or session-scoped value. A live-writer computing an id for the same path
     (using the identical `node_id_for` derivation — a small shared helper, not a protocol to reinvent)
     converges on the SAME node the batch sweep would produce. This holds across a DERIVATION change too, not
     just concurrent writers: the sweep's identity-reconcile ([CXC-DS-NODE-ID]) re-keys any node whose stored id
     predates a `node_id_for` case/format change, so two writers — or two code versions — never duplicate a node
     for one file. (Asserting this "by construction" WITHOUT the reconcile is exactly what let the lowercase->
     uppercase migration mint case-variant twins: the dedup must key on IDENTITY, never on the id string.)
  2. **Existing-node touches are already mechanical-only.** [CXC-DS-DIFF]/[CXC-DS-FIELD-OWNERSHIP] already
     forbid the batch app from writing anything but `hash`/`modified`/`missing`/`missing-since` on a node that
     already exists — this was required by the perceive/act split regardless of a second writer, so a
     live-monitor's semantic enrichment (or an Intelligence's) is NEVER at risk of being clobbered by a later
     full reconcile: the reconcile only ever re-touches the same four mechanical keys, in place, and leaves
     every other key (semantic or otherwise) exactly as found — this is the schema-tolerant pass-through
     ([CXC-DS-C7]) doing double duty as the merge-safety guarantee.
  3. **Writes are atomic.** `save()` renders to a temp file in the ledger's directory and `os.replace()`s it
     into place — a reader (or a live-monitor mid-append) never observes a torn/partial ledger file. This does
     NOT by itself serialize two concurrent writers (a real lock/queue is a live-monitor-side concern, out of
     scope here) but it guarantees each individual write is all-or-nothing.
- reserved, not built: a bus namespace `composeio/deliverable/new` is RESERVED for the future live-monitor to
  publish on when it appends a node in real time (mirroring the `self/loose_ends/*` convention session_sweep's
  `--bus` mode uses). This batch app publishes nothing — it stays one-shot/no-bus per its brief — this is a
  documented seam, not a dependency.
- change: "Added atomic (temp+replace) writes and named the path-keyed-id + mechanical-only-touch properties
  explicitly as the multi-writer contract, in response to the coordinator flagging a planned live companion."
  context_for_change:
    prior_state: "The spec already had path-keyed ids and mechanical-only touches as consequences of the
      perceive/act split, but didn't name them as a MULTI-WRITER safety property, and writes were a plain
      `write_text` (not torn-read-safe)."
    reasoning: "Two independent processes writing one file needs the invariants stated explicitly (so a future
      builder of the live-monitor doesn't have to re-derive them) and needs the one cheap hardening (atomic
      replace) this app can add unilaterally without knowing anything about the monitor's own design."
    invalidating_assumption: "If the live-monitor needs true concurrent-write serialization (both processes
      writing within the same second), a lock file or single-writer-queue becomes necessary — atomic replace
      alone only prevents torn reads, not last-write-wins data loss between two near-simultaneous writers. That
      design is deferred to the live-monitor's own spec, not solved here."
    implication: "No behavior change for this batch app's current one-shot use; purely additive hardening."
- test: `save()` never leaves a `.tmp` file behind on success; a save interrupted mid-write (simulated by
  killing before `os.replace`) leaves the PRIOR ledger content intact, never a truncated file.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

---

### [CXC-DS-REALTIME-PERSISTS] Every Realtime Detector Persists to the Ledger — Not Just Emits

- definition: The durable Artifacts surfaces (the rail card, the Artifacts-tab history view) render the
  `self/deliverables` bus projection, which is a pure projection of the LEDGER (`deliverable_publisher`
  reads the ledger file and nothing else). Therefore ANY realtime deliverable detector — the transcript
  monitor (my Write/Edit) AND the filesystem watcher (David's EXTERNAL editor writes) — MUST persist the
  change it detects to the ledger (via the shared `run_sweep(write=True)`, the node-keyed + mechanical-only
  + atomic co-writer path this section makes safe) BEFORE (or as) it emits the transient `deliverable/new`
  / `deliverable/modified` banner alert. A detector that only EMITS surfaces a fleeting banner and then the
  deliverable is invisible on every durable surface until the next batch sweep — the reported failure mode
  for `bootstrap/dusk-gifts/away-watch-2026-07-25-report.md`, which the filesystem watcher detected (banner
  fired) but never wrote, so `self/deliverables` never carried it and neither the rail card nor the
  Artifacts tab ever showed it.
- invariant: emit-without-persist is a defect. The alert and the ledger write are ONE act, not two — the
  banner is the ephemeral echo of a durable ledger row, never a substitute for one.
- rationale: the watcher exists solely to catch David's external edits (the transcript monitor is blind to
  them); if that path does not persist, David's own deliverables are exactly the ones that never reach the
  durable Artifacts view. Reuse-by-reference: both detectors call the SAME `run_sweep` the batch app owns —
  never a second ad-hoc ledger-append implementation ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]).
- test: a filesystem watcher poll that detects a new/changed tracked file, given a real ledger, leaves that
  file present as a ledger node (`missing: false`) — so a subsequent `deliverable_publisher` projection
  carries it in `self/deliverables.all`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

---

## 6. CLI

### [CXC-DS-CLI] `python -m composeio.deliverable_sweep`

```
python -m composeio.deliverable_sweep sweep --ledger PATH [--root DIR ...] [--write] [--json] [--session KEY]
python -m composeio.deliverable_sweep --selftest
```

- `sweep` is the default/only subcommand (mirrors session_sweep's flat CLI); `--root` may repeat to sweep
  multiple project roots in one invocation (each becomes a `project-root` entry, and is ADDED to the ledger's
  configured `project-roots` list on `--write` if not already present — additive, never removes a root a human
  configured).
- `--root` with no ledger config yet seeds `project-roots` + the tuned filter defaults ([CXC-DS-FILTER-DEFAULTS])
  into a fresh ledger on first `--write`.
- `--json` emits the machine report (§8) instead of the ASCII readout; combinable with dry-run (no `--write`)
  for bootstrap consumption.
- `--session KEY` — see [CXC-DS-FIELD-OWNERSHIP]'s conditionally-mechanical `generated-in-session`.
- exit code: 0 always for a successful sweep (findings are data, not failure) except a hard error (bad ledger
  path's parent missing, unreadable file) which exits 2, matching session_sweep's convention.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

---

## 7. The Two Integration Points

### [CXC-DS-BOOTSTRAP] Bootstrap: Harness Sweeps, Hands the Intelligence the Delta

- definition: at session start, the harness runs `sweep --ledger deliverables/deliverable-ledger.lts --json`
  (dry-run, no `--write` — bootstrap should never silently mutate the ledger before the Intelligence has seen
  the delta) and surfaces the `--json` report to the Intelligence, which reports NEW/MODIFIED/REMOVED counts
  and highlights to David per the existing "which .jsonl context files were loaded"-style session-start
  reporting convention. If David wants the ledger actually updated, the Intelligence (or the harness, on
  David's standing instruction) re-invokes with `--write`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-SESSION-END] Session-End: Sweep Points at New/Changed Entries to Enrich

- definition: at session end (dusk), the flow is `sweep --write --session <current-session-key> --json` — this
  DOES persist (mechanical fields for anything new/changed/removed this session), and the `--json` output's
  `needs-enrichment` list is exactly the node ids the Intelligence should now fill: for each, replace the
  `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` literal in `kind`/`topics`/`summary`/`target-audience`/`confidentiality`/`review-outcome`/
  `lifecycle-position` (and `generated-in-session` if still stamped) with real values, by directly editing
  those `- key: value` lines in the ledger's `## Deliverables` section (the app's own writer never overwrites
  a value that isn't the literal `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT`, so a hand-enriched field surviving a later `--write` is
  preserved by construction — re-running the sweep on a MODIFIED file only touches `hash`/`modified`, never a
  semantic field, per [CXC-DS-FIELD-OWNERSHIP]).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

### [CXC-DS-REPORT-FORMAT] The `--json` Report Schema

```json
{
  "ledger": "deliverables/deliverable-ledger.lts",
  "run": {"date": "ISO-8601", "new": 2, "modified": 1, "removed": 1},
  "new":      [{"id": "DELIV-...", "path": "...", "project-root": "...", "format": "md"}],
  "modified": [{"id": "DELIV-...", "path": "...", "modified": "ISO-date"}],
  "removed":  [{"id": "DELIV-...", "path": "...", "missing-since": "ISO-8601"}],
  "needs-enrichment": ["DELIV-...", "..."]
}
```
- `needs-enrichment` is every node (new OR modified, this run) that still carries at least one literal
  `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` value after the write — the Intelligence's exact worklist. A dry-run (no `--write`)
  reports the same shape computed against the in-memory would-be state, without persisting it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DS]

---

## 8. Compliance

| ID | Requirement | Testable Criterion |
|----|-------------|-------------------|
| CXC-DS-C1 | NEW detected + mechanical fields + enrichment stamps | Synthetic new file gets path/project-root/format/created/hash correct and all 7 (or 8 with no `--session`) semantic fields == `NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` |
| CXC-DS-C2 | MODIFIED bumps `modified` only | Editing a swept file's bytes changes `hash` and `modified`; `created` and all semantic fields are untouched |
| CXC-DS-C3 | REMOVED flagged, not deleted | Deleting a swept file leaves its node in the ledger with `missing: true`, unchanged `lifecycle-position`, reported every run until resolved |
| CXC-DS-C4 | Code / do-not-track excluded | A `.py` file inside an included directory, and a file under a do-not-track dir/suffix, never appear in the ledger |
| CXC-DS-C5 | Determinism | Same tree + same ledger + same injected `now` -> byte-identical rendered ledger and `--json` report, across repeated runs |
| CXC-DS-C6 | Never guesses semantics | No code path assigns a non-`NEEDS-ENRICHMENT` value to any of the 7 semantic fields |
| CXC-DS-C7 | Schema-tolerant | An unrecognized property already present on a hand-enriched node round-trips through `--write` unchanged (not dropped, not rejected) |
| CXC-DS-C8 | Dry-run safety | Without `--write`, the ledger file (and its mtime) is untouched, regardless of what the delta contains |
| CXC-DS-C9 | Multi-writer safe | `save()` writes atomically (temp+replace); a full reconcile touches only mechanical fields on a pre-existing node, never its semantic fields — safe substrate for a future live-monitor co-writer |
| CXC-DS-C10 | Extraction-clean | Zero `import composeio...` in the package; every root/filter/ledger-path/frontmatter value is config/CLI-driven, none hardcoded |
| CXC-DS-C11 | Pending-enrichment accuracy | A fresh NEW node's `pending-enrichment` lists all 8 enrichable fields; after hand-enriching 2 of them and triggering a real MODIFIED, it recomputes to the remaining 6 |

